From af8702167d5c67c36bf0d2c419d4bcdd57417fdb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 10:12:59 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] WebPlayer: play the web client's audio through Kodi's audio
 engine

A web page's audio left through whatever sink the client's GStreamer
found, beside Kodi: Kodi's volume, mute, device choice and passthrough
settings did not apply to it. Serve a socket the client's audio sink
connects to and play each sink as a stream of Kodi's audio engine:
interleaved PCM of up to eight channels, and AC3, E-AC3 and DTS through
the passthrough path where the output allows it. Kodi tells the sink
which PCM encodings it takes, both byte orders and both 24-bit
packings among them, converting what its engine does not take as is,
and the rates at which each compressed format passes through. The sink
sends compressed audio as IEC 61937 bursts so its clock runs at the
burst rate, and reads the engine's delay from a shared page, so the
page's video keeps in step with what is heard. kodi-web-audio.h
describes the protocol.

Signed-off-by: Daniel Golle <daniel@makrotopia.org>
